Primary driver: Çalışkan YK, Başak F, Erdem O. "When algorithms triage trauma: Diagnostic accuracy,
undertriage risk, and prompt fragility in frontier large language models." Surgery 2026;196:110319.
doi 10.1016/j.surg.2026.110319, PMID 42214316. Peer-reviewed; PubMed-verified 22 Oct 2026.
University of Health Sciences, Istanbul. Reference standard: consensus adjudication by
board-certified surgeons using Injury Severity Score-anchored categories.

Design: 150 trauma vignettes, three frontier families (GPT-5.2, Gemini 3.0 Flash, Claude 4.5 Opus),
locked prompt template, Feb 2026. Primary outcome binary major vs non-major trauma.

Findings:
  - Strong aggregate performance: sensitivity 89.4%, specificity 86.5%, pooled kappa 0.74
    (GPT-5.2 AUROC 0.94 / kappa 0.82; Gemini 0.91/0.75; Claude 0.89/0.65).
  - **Undertriage clustered rather than scattering: geriatric 17.6% vs 5.2% in younger adults
    (P = .012); blunt 11.8% vs penetrating 4.1% (P = .045).**
  - **"Across standardized lexical perturbations applied to all 150 vignettes, 11.2% of repeated
    prompts changed triage category, with a maximum instability of 24.5%."**
  - Simulated delay from undertriage affected 14.2% of major-trauma cases, mean 18.5 minutes.
  - Authors' framing: "high aggregate accuracy may conceal clinically meaningful subgroup errors …
    Their errors were not random; they clustered in older adults and less overt trauma patterns."

