EDIT 1 — SPECIFICATION/INTERPRETATION. The register contrast needs a
paraphrase-invariance floor, and this revision measures none
Section affected: §5.2 (scenario architecture — dual register)

The gap in OUR protocol, and it sits on the study's signature feature. §5.2 declares the dual
register "a deliberate distribution-shift probe, **not a stylistic variant**." That is an assertion
about what the contrast measures. A grep of the protocol for perturbation, paraphrase, lexical,
wording, rephrasing, or invariance returns **nothing** — the only matches are unrelated remarks about
agreement coefficients. There is no paraphrase control anywhere.

Why that matters now rather than in principle: Çalışkan et al. show frontier models flipping triage
category at 11.2% (max 24.5%) under rewording that holds clinical content fixed. If an observed
lay-versus-expert difference is of comparable magnitude, it is **not identifiable against a
lexical-noise floor the study has not measured**. The register factor is the protocol's distinctive
claim, and it is currently resting on an unmeasured assumption.

This is a different quantity from anything the protocol already reports, and the distinction is the
whole point:
  - **Run-to-run instability** (§4.2) is same-input nondeterminism — identical prompt, repeated.
  - The **any-run critical-miss rate** proposed on 24 September addresses the tail-blindness of
    aggregating those runs.
  - A **paraphrase-invariance floor** is same-content, *different-wording* instability. None of the
    three substitutes for the others, and only the third bounds the register effect.

Scope discipline — what this edit does NOT do. It adds no measurement, no scenario, no model call,
no participant burden, and has **no bearing on the §8.4 power calculation**. It states the
interpretive constraint and narrows the §5.2 claim so it rests on how the two forms are constructed
rather than on a measurement the study does not make. The measurement itself is surfaced below as an
option and is NOT proposed.

Honesty about transfer, written into the text: Çalışkan is trauma secondary triage with a
surgeon-adjudicated reference and a locked prompt — not lay-register patient-facing triage. The
perturbation magnitude is cited as evidence that a floor exists and is non-trivial, not as an
estimate of our floor.

