NOT PROPOSED — recorded so the reasoning is auditable

1. SUBGROUP CLUSTERING OF UNDERTRIAGE — flagged, not proposed. Çalışkan found undertriage
   concentrated in geriatric cases (17.6% vs 5.2%, P = .012) and blunt mechanisms (11.8% vs 4.1%,
   P = .045), concluding the errors "were not random." A grep of this protocol for geriatric, older
   adult, subgroup, or stratified analysis returns **nothing**: under- and over-triage are reported
   by ESI boundary (Table A4) and severity weight, but no patient-characteristic subgroup analysis is
   pre-specified, so an aggregate Critical Miss Rate could conceal exactly this pattern.
   Why no edit: whether the 50-scenario set carries enough age variation to support a stratified
   analysis is a design-and-power question, not a drafting one, and §8.4 power is the blocking item.
   Pre-specifying a subgroup analysis the study cannot power would be worse than stating none.
   **This belongs with the PI's open design decisions, and I would raise it when §8.4 is addressed.**

2. Wang YH. "The Performance Ceiling of LIDC-IDRI Is Label-Defined Rather than Image-Defined."
   J Imaging Inform Med 2026. doi 10.1007/s10278-026-02420-1, PMID 42897425. PubMed-verified.
   Flagged as a methodological observation, NOT proposed. The paper proves that against a
   reader-panel-derived label no model can exceed the AUC of the latent propensity itself, that the
   ceiling depends on the labelling policy (0.898-0.913 retaining all nodules; 0.946-0.954
   discarding reader-split cases; 0.991-0.994 unanimous-only), and that 11 of 27 audited published
   figures exceed the bound implied by each study's own declared rule.
   How it does and does not apply to us: our gold standard is physician-panel-derived, so a
   label-defined ceiling exists in principle. But the obvious failure mode does NOT apply — §7.5
   resolves disagreement by "modified Delphi discussion until consensus or supermajority" rather
   than discarding split items, so the protocol does not inflate its ceiling by dropping the hard
   cases. What the paper suggests is subtler: a supermajority-resolved label is a noisier label than
   a unanimous one, and unless **label provenance is recorded per item** the analysis cannot report
   performance against label reliability. Whether that provenance is already captured I could not
   establish from the protocol text. Worth a look, but transporting a single-author mathematical
   argument from lung-nodule imaging into a triage protocol is not something I will do on my own
   initiative.

